#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084374} :activity/id #uuid "3807724b-f201-5101-ab81-a4cf26b5a6fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050587 :topic/id #uuid "5608f963-4aa6-51e0-b27f-7406f85dcd4a" :topic/math-academy-id 794} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084508 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a bag of tiles labeled $a,b,c,d,e$, and $f$, where the bag contains an equal number of each tile. We draw a single tile at random from the bag. The sample space $\\mathcal S$ of all possible outcomes is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = \\{a, b, c, d, e, f \\}\n$$\n\nLet's define two events, $A$ and $B$, as follows:\n\n- Let $A$ be the event that we draw a vowel. Then, we have\n$A = \\{a, e \\}$.\n- Let $B$ be the event that we get one of the first three letters of the alphabet. Then, we have\n$B = \\{a, b, c \\}$.\n\nHow do we calculate $P(A\\cap B)$?\n\nLet's use a Venn diagram representation for $A\\cap B$, as shown below. Notice that we haven't included the probabilities, just the values contained within $\\mathcal S$.\n\n![](images/02/027955ed4e60b71c008c054c29c47bdaf092c4f4aff255327ea3c5bda7788f60.png)\n\nLet's construct another Venn diagram in which we count the number of entries in each region of the Venn diagram above.\n\n![](images/43/43790d39be47480338fdd4df095854de3757b36ce9bc659d60f9b3a6e8688269.png)\n\nWe're told that there is an equal number of each tile, so every outcome in the sample space is equally likely. Therefore, to compute $P(A \\cap B)$, we need to count the number of events in $A \\cap B$ and divide by the total number of events in $\\mathcal S$.\n\n- In a Venn diagram, $A \\cap B$ is represented by the area where the circles overlap. From the diagram above, we see that there is only $\\color{red}1$ event in $A \\cap B$.\n- The total number of events in $\\mathcal S$ is $1+1+2+2={\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = \\frac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region representing the event we're interested in and divide by the total number of outcomes in the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n\nFinally, it is important to remember that this method works *only* if every outcome in the sample space is equally likely." :tutorial/id #uuid "8155ae13-9be9-576a-a096-d3c633e6e865" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0419327a-0fde-5cc1-b922-39de54c895bb" :step/math-academy-id 59918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082919 :question/id #uuid "6616ee7f-30ed-54ef-b578-b33bdb0101bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-13070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6820924-ae0c-5bd0-b374-ef7b5badcc47" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048135}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083698}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Venn Diagram Given a Description of a Sample Space"} :step/id #uuid "2675beda-8eb1-5e62-a656-8901b8c74f2a" :step/math-academy-id 59919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084376}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082920 :question/id #uuid "ad342550-d410-529f-a5c0-5b67845cff81" :question/math-academy-id "e-13071"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd8cd17-5aed-557e-b301-9828d84745c3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083696}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "52b66831-dec9-58df-b2e0-185b933ab681" :step/math-academy-id 59922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :question/id #uuid "fcdd0c3b-36f8-5452-820a-114a8e70aa49" :question/math-academy-id "e-13212"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9401702-a2d9-5c53-a3de-8109a75111d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083578}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space"} :step/id #uuid "41926e22-9aaf-53ef-ae46-08eca29ed619" :step/math-academy-id 59925}] :activity/title "Computing Probabilities for Compound Events Using Venn Diagrams" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083471}]}
